---
// Data study built from the whole corpus. Targets head terms ("online store red
// flags", "is domain age a good signal") rather than the long-tail store
// queries, and is the kind of page that earns citations — which is what the
// store pages need in order to rank at all.
//
// Every figure is derived from data/stats.json at build time, so rerunning
// `npm run stats` and rebuilding keeps the page honest as the corpus grows.
// Nothing here is hardcoded.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import stats from '../../../data/stats.json';

const n = stats.counts.indexable;
const fmt = (x: number) => x.toLocaleString('en-US');
const pctOf = (part: number) => ((part / n) * 100).toFixed(1);

const age = stats.domainAge;
const social = stats.social;
const policy = stats.policyPages;
const buckets = stats.correlationAgeVsTrust.filter((b: any) => b.n > 0);
const r = stats.correlationAgeScorePearson.r;

const held = policy.countHeldDistribution as Record<string, number>;
const heldNone = held['0'] ?? 0;
const heldAll = held['4'] ?? 0;

const youngest = buckets[0];
const oldest = buckets[buckets.length - 1];

// Platform mix matters for reading the rest: this is not a random sample of
// all e-commerce, and saying so up front is what makes the numbers defensible.
const platformTop = Object.entries(stats.platform)
  .map(([name, v]: [string, any]) => ({ name, ...v }))
  .sort((a, b) => b.n - a.n)
  .slice(0, 4);

const socialPlatforms = Object.entries(social.perPlatformPresent)
  .map(([name, v]: [string, any]) => ({ name, ...v }))
  .sort((a, b) => b.pct - a.pct);

const checkedOn = String(stats.generatedAt).slice(0, 10);
const title = `What ${fmt(n)} Online Stores Reveal About Trust Signals`;
const description = `We ran automated trust checks on ${fmt(n)} independent online stores. Domain age turns out to be a weak predictor, and newer stores publish more policy pages than mid-life ones.`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: title,
  description,
  datePublished: checkedOn,
  dateModified: checkedOn,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'CheckIfLegit' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'CheckIfLegit' },
  about: 'Trust signals of online stores',
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <h1>What {fmt(n)} Online Stores Reveal About Trust Signals</h1>

  <p>
    Most advice about spotting a dubious online store repeats the same rules of thumb:
    check how old the domain is, look for a returns policy, see whether they have social
    accounts. We had the chance to test those rules against data rather than intuition —
    we run automated public-signal checks on {fmt(n)} independent online stores, and this
    is what the corpus actually shows.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two of the findings cut against the standard advice. Read the
    <a href="#methodology">methodology and limitations</a> before quoting any of it;
    several of these numbers measure what an automated checker can <em>see</em>, which is
    not the same as what exists.
  </p>

  <h2 id="age">Finding 1: domain age is a weak predictor on its own</h2>
  <p>
    "Check the domain age" is the most repeated piece of advice in this space. Across
    {fmt(stats.correlationAgeScorePearson.n)} stores where we could read a WHOIS creation
    date, the correlation between domain age and our overall trust score is
    <strong>r = {r}</strong> — positive, but weak. Age carries real information at the
    extremes and very little in the middle.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Domain age</th><th>Stores</th><th>Mean trust score</th>
        <th>No social presence</th><th>Avg. policy pages (of 4)</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {buckets.map((b: any) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{b.bucket}</td>
          <td>{fmt(b.n)}</td>
          <td>{b.meanScore}</td>
          <td>{b.pctNoSocial}%</td>
          <td>{b.avgPolicyPagesOf4}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The mean score climbs steeply from {youngest.meanScore} for domains under six months
    to {buckets[2]?.meanScore} by the one-to-two-year mark, then flattens — the gap between
    a two-year-old store and a ten-year-old one is only about
    {(oldest.meanScore - buckets[3].meanScore).toFixed(1)} points. A brand-new domain is
    worth extra care. A three-year-old domain is not meaningfully safer than an
    eleven-year-old one, and treating age as a headline signal will mislead you in both
    directions.
  </p>

  <h2 id="policies">Finding 2: newer stores publish <em>more</em> policy pages, not fewer</h2>
  <p>
    The intuition is that young stores cut corners on paperwork. The corpus says the
    opposite: stores under six months old carry an average of
    {youngest.avgPolicyPagesOf4} of the four standard policy pages, more than every
    other age bracket including the {oldest.bucket} group at {oldest.avgPolicyPagesOf4}.
  </p>
  <p>
    The likely explanation is not virtue but defaults. Modern store platforms ship
    privacy, terms, refund and shipping templates out of the box, so a store built last
    month starts with them; one built a decade ago on an older stack often never added
    them. That makes policy-page presence a decent signal of <em>platform vintage</em>
    and a poor signal of trustworthiness. Note the small sample in that youngest
    bracket ({fmt(youngest.n)} stores), which is why we would not lean on it hard.
  </p>

  <h2 id="social">Finding 3: absent social presence tracks age strongly</h2>
  <p>
    Social presence is the signal that separates age brackets most cleanly.
    {youngest.pctNoSocial}% of stores under six months old link to no major social
    platform at all, falling steadily to {oldest.pctNoSocial}% among domains over ten
    years old. Across the whole corpus, {social.pctNone}% link to none.
  </p>
  <p>Where the {fmt(social.measured)} measured stores do have a presence:</p>
  <ul>
    {socialPlatforms.map((p) => (
      <li>{p.name.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + p.name.slice(1)} — {p.pct}%</li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <p>
    The TikTok figure ({socialPlatforms.find((p) => p.name === 'tiktok')?.pct}%) is worth
    noting given how much attention TikTok-advertised storefronts get in scam coverage:
    they are a real phenomenon but a small share of independent e-commerce overall.
  </p>

  <h2 id="coverage">Finding 4: complete policy coverage is the exception</h2>
  <p>
    Only {fmt(heldAll)} stores ({pctOf(heldAll)}%) publish all four standard policy pages
    where our checker can find them, while {fmt(heldNone)} ({pctOf(heldNone)}%) have none
    we could detect. By type:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Page</th><th>Not detected</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {Object.entries(policy.perType).map(([type, v]: [string, any]) => (
        <tr><td>{type}</td><td>{v.pctMissing}%</td></tr>
      ))}
      <tr><td>contact page</td><td>{stats.contactPage.pctMissingOfMeasured}%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Refund and shipping policies are missing far more often than privacy and terms,
    which is the reverse of what a shopper cares about — privacy policies are driven by
    regulation, whereas the pages that tell you what happens when an order goes wrong are
    the ones most often absent. If you check one thing before buying from an unfamiliar
    store, make it the returns policy.
  </p>

  <h2 id="methodology">Methodology and limitations</h2>
  <p>
    We checked {fmt(n)} independent online stores, each fetched once. Signals are read
    from the homepage plus a small set of conventional policy-page paths. Data as of
    {checkedOn}. Figures update when the corpus is re-analysed.
  </p>
  <p><strong>These limitations genuinely constrain the numbers above:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Detection is not existence.</strong> Every "missing" figure means
      <em>not discoverable by an automated check of the homepage and common paths</em>.
      A policy linked only from a checkout flow, rendered by JavaScript after load, or
      placed at an unconventional URL reads as absent here. Treat these as floors on
      what exists, not counts of what does not.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Language coverage is partial.</strong> Policy-page detection recognises
      English, German, French, Spanish and Italian naming conventions. Stores operating
      in other languages — Dutch, Polish, Russian, Portuguese and Vietnamese all appear
      in this corpus — are undercounted, so the true coverage rates are higher than
      reported.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Two signals are excluded entirely.</strong> Our physical-address check only
      reliably matches English street-address formats, so it under-detects non-English
      sites by roughly threefold and is not reported here. Third-party review lookups
      were blocked for effectively the whole corpus, so review presence is also excluded.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>WHOIS coverage is incomplete.</strong> A creation date was available for
      {fmt(age.measured)} of {fmt(n)} domains ({(100 - age.pctUnknown).toFixed(1)}%);
      age findings cover only those.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The sample is not representative of all e-commerce.</strong> It skews
      heavily toward one platform ({platformTop[0].name} at {platformTop[0].pct}%) and
      toward independent stores rather than marketplaces or
      large retailers. Median domain age is {age.medianAgeYears} years and only
      {age.under1yr.pctOfMeasured}% are under a year old, so this is a corpus of mostly
      established small stores.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Correlation, not causation.</strong> The trust score is computed from
      several of the same signals broken out in these tables, so the age/score
      correlation is not independent evidence.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Related</h2>
  <p>
    See also <a href="/research/shopify-vs-woocommerce">Shopify vs WooCommerce: trust
    signals compared</a>, or all <a href="/research/">research</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Browse the underlying pages</h2>
  <p>
    Every store in this analysis has its own signal breakdown. Browse the full
    <a href="/stores/">A–Z index</a>, or by
    <a href="/platform/">platform</a>, <a href="/tld/">country</a>, or
    <a href="/verdict/">trust level</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 1.25rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: 0.5rem 0.65rem; border-bottom: 1px solid #e5e7eb; }
  th { font-weight: 600; }
  td:not(:first-child), th:not(:first-child) { text-align: right; }
  /* Wide tables scroll rather than pushing the page sideways on mobile. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) { table { display: block; overflow-x: auto; } }
</style>
